WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:05.680
One of the most common types of unit tests is&nbsp;
to test a function to see if it performs as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.040
should. For us to test a function correctly,&nbsp;
we need to know what results we expect to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.200
when testing said function. For this example,&nbsp;
I will use the function we built elsewhere in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:22.360
the course to run some tests. Testing a function&nbsp;
involves anticipating known results and testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:28.800
the function to see if the expected results&nbsp;
are returned. In this 532-functions file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:36.440
we have a function defined on line 2 that&nbsp;
has two arguments: score and multiplier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:43.680
The function is used to calculate a game&nbsp;
player’s total score. Line 3 returns the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:52.560
score multiplied by the multiplier. On lines&nbsp;
5 and 6, we have two tests for this function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:59.920
and to the right of each test is a comment&nbsp;
with an expected value. I will run this code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:07.240
and we see the two expected values. If the&nbsp;
tests did not work, we would need to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:13.160
either at the data being used to test the function&nbsp;
or the logic of the function itself. Functions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:18.240
like all other code, should be tested before&nbsp;
an app is put in production, as having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:27.880
incorrect function results can lead to financial,&nbsp;
statistical, and credibility issues within an app.

